[38;5;231m⏺[39m [1mSection 3 — Tooling and testing[0m

  The repo has no test runner, no linter, no formatter, and no [38;5;153mscripts[39m block. So this is a choice, not an existing
  pattern to follow.

  What's actually worth testing here is Section 1, not Section 2. The failure behavior has four real branches — defaults
  merge, corrupt JSON, throwing storage, unknown theme value — and every one of them is a silent fallback, meaning a
  regression produces no error, just quietly wrong behavior. That's the kind of code that needs tests. The DOM wiring in
  Section 2 is thin and mostly not worth automating.

  Testing [38;5;153mprefs.js[39m in Node is cheaper than it first looks: it's a classic script that assigns a global, so a test can
  define a fake [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m and [38;5;153mrequire[39m the file directly. Node's built-in [38;5;153mnode:test[39m covers it with [1mzero dependencies[0m —
  which keeps the current [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m shape (no [38;5;153mdependencies[39m, no [38;5;153mdevDependencies[39m) intact. It needs one [38;5;153m"test": "node [39m
  [38;5;153m--test"[39m script.

  A linter/formatter is the other candidate. It's cheapest to add before the code exists rather than after, but it means
  the first real [38;5;153mdevDependencies[39m in a repo that has deliberately had none, and it would flag the existing [38;5;153mapp.js[39m and
  [38;5;153msrc/[39m too — which is scope beyond this task.

  My recommendation: [1munit tests via [38;5;153mnode:test[39m, no linter.[0m The tests pay for themselves against silent-fallback logic;
  the linter's value on ~100 lines of new code is mostly style, and it drags unrelated files into the change.
